"], "examples": [], "quotes": [{"text": "Willowing over the rough cobbles of the little pier stepped a thin, bent figure, adorned with a silver nanny-goat's beard and bobbling eyes interrupted by the rim of a pair of pince-nez.", "reference": "1928, Robert Byron, “Gardenias and Sweetpeas”, in The Station: Athos: Treasures and Men, 1st American edition, New York, N.Y.: Alfred A[braham] Knopf, →OCLC, page 175:"}, {"text": "Joe’s impulse was to sketch her, with her shadow willowing beyond her on the mouse-gray paving-stone; but his left fist, obeying instinct, remained clenched behind his back […]", "reference": "1930, Talbot Mundy, chapter 7, in Black Light:"}, {"text": "It was floating a foot under the surface. The eyes were holes. The mouth was a slit cavern of darkness. The hair willowed around the scalp.", "reference": "1985, Martin Booth, Hiroshima Joe, New York: Picador, page 394:"}, {"text": "The draft-drawn smoke willowed down through the hole and across my face, but I didn’t worry about coughing or sneezing.", "reference": "2013, Dean Koontz, Wilderness, Bantam Books:"}], "synonyms": [], "antonyms": [], "translations": [], "subsenses": []}], "synonyms": [], "antonyms": []}], "source": {"url": "https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/willow", "license": {"name": "CC BY-SA 4.0", "url": "https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/4.0/"}}}}